Wed Oct 28 11:23:13 UTC 2026 - Tina Müller <timueller+perl@suse.de>

- updated to 5.522.0 (5.522)
   see /usr/share/doc/packages/perl-MIME-tools/ChangeLog

  5.522	  2026-10-27  Dianne Skoll <dianne@skoll.ca>
  	* VERSION 5.522 RELEASED
  	* Many security issues were addressed (see the following ten CVEs)
  	  Issues were brought to my attention, and patches supplied, by the
  	  CPANSec security group.
  	* parse_params: flag a parameter list that could not be parsed
  	  Detect additional ambiguous parse situations when parameter
  	  names contain illegal characters.  Fixes:
  	  CVE-2026-116264 invalid parameter hides boundary/filename. bsc#1288723
  	* parse_params: check the boundary after RFC 2231 reassembly
  	  Another ambiguous-parse case was missed (trailing space after
  	  boundary was not detected for RFC-2231-encoded boundaries.)  Fixes:
  	  CVE-2026-116265 RFC 2231 boundary skips ambiguity checks. bsc#1288722
  	* parse_params: Stricter checking for encoded boundaries; earlier
  	  versions would miss some cases.  Fixes:
  	  CVE-2026-116266 boundary changed by decoding not flagged. bsc#1288721
  	* Cap the indentation levels in the Results: structure.  Otherwise,
  	  specially-crafted messages could consume large amounts of memory.
  	  Fixes: CVE-2026-116267 indent stored per message per level bsc#1288720
  	* parse_params: stop a comment match at the next parenthesis.
  	  Otherwise, specially-crafted messages could cause the regex
  	  back-tracker to consume large amounts of CPU time.  Note that
  	  MIME::Parser does not correctly parse ((nested comments)) and
  	  has never done so.  Fixes:
  	  CVE-2026-116268 unclosed "(" comments backtrack CPU exhaustion. bsc#1288719
  	* parse_params: re-encode filename* values without charset tricks
  	  Specially-crafted messages could trick recommended_filename()
  	  into returning an empty string instead of a plausible filename.
  	  Fixes: CVE-2026-116269 RFC 2231 language tag empties filename. bsc#1288718
  	* decode_mimewords: do not copy the input into each error.  Otherwise,
  	  specially-crafted messages could consume large amounts of memory.
  	  Fixes: CVE-2026-116270 each bad "=?" copies the input into $@. bsc#1288717
  	* FileUnder: do not reuse an existing generated directory.  Otherwise,
  	  attackers might be able to use symbolic links to get files created
  	  in unexpected locations.  Fixes:
  	  CVE-2026-116271 FileUnder reuses a pre-created directory. bsc#1288716
  	* parse_params: flag comments other readers do not skip
  	  Specially-crafted messages could create ambiguous messages without
  	  setting the ambiguous_parse flag.  Fixes:
  	  CVE-2026-116272  comment hides a duplicate boundary. bsc#1288715
  	* Call whine() rather than error() if max_parts or max_depth is
  	  exceeded during a parse.  This lets us return undef rather than
  	  throwing an exception if ignore_errors() is set to 0.
  	* Change to defaults: max_parts is now set to 1000 and max_depth
  	  to 64 rather than being unlimited.  This should not affect most
  	  real-world uses of MIME::tools, but you may need to increase
  	  these values or set them to -1 (unlimited) if you run into trouble.
  	      ***** NOTE INCOMPATIBILITY *****
  	  Fixes: CVE-2026-116273 max_parts and max_depth unlimited. bsc#1288714
  	* Do not signal an ambiguous parse if we have filename="" or
  	  name=""
  	* Update author names in META.yml
  	* Add git repo to META.yml
  5.520	  2026-10-21  Dianne Skoll <dianne@skoll.ca>
  	* VERSION 5.520 RELEASED
  	* MIME::Parser used O(N^2) CPU time and memory parsing a MIME
  	  message nested N levels deep.  A smallish specially-crafted
  	  message could DOS the computer by making it consume enormous
  	  amounts of memory and CPU time.  The parser was modified to use
  	  O(N) time and memory in this case.  Additionally, a new
  	  MIME::Parser::max_depth() function was added to limit the nesting
  	  depth before declaring a parse failure.  Problem was reported to
  	  me by Zhaoyang Li and Xiang Li from AOSP Lab at Nankai University.
  	* MIME::Parser would ignore multiple blank lines in a message
    	  preamble when parsing it; this could break DKIM.  Problem pointed
  	  out and fix suggested by Tim Henderson.
  	* If a MIME boundary includes trailing whitespace, we mark the
  	  parse as ambiguous.  Problem noted and patch submitted by
  	  Christian Herdtweck
  5.519	  2026-09-22  Dianne Skoll <dianne@skoll.ca>
  	* VERSION 5.519 RELEASED
  	* Some (buggy) software creates base64-encoded parts as a
  	  concatenation of multiple base64-encoded streams, and some client
  	  software decodes each stream separately and concatenates the
  	  results.  Duplicate this behavior in MIME::tools to prevent it
  	  from being used as a mechanism to sneak dangerous content past a
  	  security scanner.  Patch courtesy of Giorgio Premi
  	  <giosh94mhz@gmail.com>.

